<body>
<header style="background: transparent; border-top: none; border-left: none; border-right: none; box-shadow: none; padding: 0 0 24px 0; color: inherit; display: block;">
    <h1>A General Education in Mathematics:&nbsp;<span class="subtitle">The Middle Way</span></h1>
  </header>

  <p><strong style="color: rgb(30, 41, 59); font-size: medium;">Middle Way Mathematics (MWM)</strong><span style="color: rgb(30, 41, 59); font-size: medium;"> is a curriculum and foundational framework for a general education in mathematics. The curriculum targets all motivated students, providing a clear overview of the nature of modern mathematical thought. It is not designed to prepare students for technical STEM tracks; rather, its goal is to provide a conceptual and formal literacy that enables lifelong, thoughtful engagement with formal descriptions - offered in more sophisticated popular science literature and video lectures - of modern physical theories.</span></p>

  <h2>1. Mathematics as a Formal Science</h2>

  <p>
    Mathematics is treated as a formal science whose home is a world of supposition. It serves as a bridge linking human intuition with empirical experience—an arena where we ask <em>"What if?"</em>, trace deductive consequences, and evaluate how effectively the resulting structures model physical observations, as illustrated.</p>

